{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM"],
    /*
     * On, like the root projects. This declares its own options rather than
     * extending `tsconfig.base.json`, for the `paths` reason given below — which
     * meant the claim that the repository is strict throughout had a hole in the
     * one project written to outlive framework upgrades.
     *
     * Turning it on reported 94 errors: 57 possibly-null or possibly-undefined,
     * 28 implicit-`any` index reads, and 9 argument and overload mismatches.
     * Almost all of them were one shape — a spec reaching into
     * `driver.dataContext.instanceFullData`, which is `InstanceObject | null`,
     * and then indexing an `InstanceNode` union that a key cannot address. The
     * fix is `harness/src/nodes.ts`, which navigates through the model's own
     * guards and fails with the path when the shape is not what the spec claims.
     */
    "strict": true,
    "experimentalDecorators": true,
    "emitDecoratorMetadata": false,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,
    /*
     * Kept here, unlike in the root projects, and for a reason that does not
     * expire: `paths` below points `@angular/core` at a local stub. Every `.d.ts`
     * in node_modules that imports from `@angular/core` — `@ngx-translate` first
     * among them — then resolves against the stub and reports missing exports it
     * was never meant to have. Checking those declarations would measure the stub
     * against Angular's API, which is the opposite of the point.
     */
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "types": ["node", "vitest/globals"],
    /*
     * No `baseUrl`: TypeScript 6 deprecates it and 7 stops honouring it, and the
     * mappings below are already written relative to this file, which is where
     * `paths` resolves from without it.
     *
     * Removing it made this project type-check for the first time. TS5101 is a
     * configuration error, and tsc reports those instead of checking the program —
     * so `tsc -p harness/tsconfig.json` reported one problem and looked clean. It
     * now reports the 25 real ones underneath, most of them the Angular stub in
     * `stubs/` not exporting what the shared code imports. Those are fixed, and
     * `npm run typecheck` runs this project in the gate — vitest itself never
     * type-checks, so without that step none of this would be enforced.
     */
    "paths": {
      "@cee/*": ["../src/app/modules/shared/*"],
      "@angular/core": ["./stubs/angular-core.ts"]
    }
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "test/**/*.ts", "stubs/**/*.ts", "vitest.config.ts"]
}
